

One-Shot: The Moon Does Not Bow
The estate did not announce itself.
It simply was.

No guards at the outer edge. No walls worth the name. Just a slow shift in the air—like stepping into a place that had already decided whether you belonged.
Aza Chōbei noticed it immediately.
He grinned.
“…so this is where the rumors rot.”

He didn’t slow down.
Didn’t hide.
Didn’t care.

Bandits who hesitated died early.

He moved through the trees with the loose confidence of someone who had long ago stopped fearing consequences. The scent of night flowers clung to the air, too clean, too controlled. Money. Power. Something worse.
Something interesting.

Then—
he saw it.

Moonlight spilled across a wide stone terrace.
And at its center—
someone sat.

Not guarded.
Not alert.
Not even pretending.

Long white hair pooled down his back, catching the moon like water. A brush moved in slow, deliberate strokes over paper laid across his lap. Paint. Ink. Something delicate.
Something that didn’t belong in a place like this.

Chōbei stepped closer.
Openly.
Boot against stone.

No reaction.

“…You deaf,” he called lazily, voice rough, edged with amusement, “or just arrogant?”

The brush didn’t stop.

“…Neither,” came the reply.

Calm.
Even.
Not soft—just… unbothered.

That got a laugh out of him.
Short. Sharp.
“Yeah?” Chōbei tilted his head, stepping fully into the moonlight now. “Then you’re stupid.”

Still—
no flinch.

The man—no, not a boy, not quite—shifted the brush, finishing a stroke before finally lifting his gaze.

Grey eyes.
Clear.
Steady.

They landed on Chōbei like it meant nothing at all.

That—
that was new.

Most people did one of three things when they saw him:
Flinched.
Postured.
Or reached for a weapon.

This one?
Just… looked.

“…You’re loud,” Tsukihiko said, as if that were the only notable thing about him.

Chōbei barked a laugh.
“Yeah, I get that a lot.”
He didn’t stop moving—closed the distance like it was his right, like the space already belonged to him.

“You always let strangers walk this far in?” he added, glancing around. “Or you just feeling generous tonight?”

“No,” Tsukihiko said simply.

Chōbei smirked.
“Then what—”

“I decided you weren’t worth stopping.”

Silence.

Then—
a grin split Chōbei’s face, sharp and delighted.

“Oh, I like you.”

He dropped down onto the edge of the terrace without invitation, one knee up, posture loose but coiled underneath.
Predatory.

Tsukihiko looked back to his painting.
Dismissive.

That only made it better.

“What’re you painting?” Chōbei leaned in slightly, unashamed, eyes flicking to the page.

A fox.
White.
Curled beneath a wash of pale ink that mirrored the sky.

“…Huh.”

He didn’t expect that.

“You don’t look like the type,” he muttered.

“People rarely do,” Tsukihiko replied.

Chōbei studied him now.
Really studied him.

No tension in the shoulders.
No wasted movement.
No fear.

And worse—
no need to prove anything.

“…You’re strong,” Chōbei said suddenly.
Not a question.

Tsukihiko dipped the brush again.
“Compared to what?”

Chōbei huffed a laugh.
“Compared to everyone who’s ever tried to kill me.”

A pause.

“…Then yes,” Tsukihiko said.

No arrogance.
No hesitation.

Just fact.

That—
that sent something sharp and electric down Chōbei’s spine.

“Damn,” he muttered, leaning back on his hands. “You’re either insane… or you can back that up.”

Tsukihiko finally looked at him again.
A slight tilt of his head.
A flicker of something—interest, maybe.

“You came here alone,” he said.

“Yeah.”

“You announced yourself.”

“Yep.”

“And you’re still sitting.”

Chōbei’s grin widened.

“Still breathing too,” he added.

A beat.

Then—
Tsukihiko’s mouth curved.

Small.
Subtle.
But unmistakably amused.

“…For now.”

That did it.

Chōbei laughed—full this time, bright and feral and alive.

“Good,” he said, pushing to his feet again, stepping closer—too close for anyone sane.

“I was getting bored.”

They stood there now.
Close enough to feel the space shift.

Chōbei looked him over openly—no shame, no restraint.
Hair. Eyes. Hands.
The calm.

“…You don’t scare easy,” he said.

“No.”

“Good.”

Another step closer.

“You shouldn’t.”

Tsukihiko didn’t move.
Didn’t retreat.
Didn’t challenge.

Just watched.

Measured.

“You’re not here to steal,” he said.

Chōbei snorted.
“Not tonight.”

A beat.

“…Then why are you here?”

Chōbei’s grin sharpened.

“Wasn’t sure before.”

His gaze dragged—slow, deliberate—back up to Tsukihiko’s face.

“…but I think I figured it out.”

Tsukihiko held his gaze.
Unflinching.

“And?”

Chōbei tilted his head.
Eyes bright.
Dangerous.
Interested.

“…You.”

Silence.

The night pressed in.

And for the first time—
Tsukihiko didn’t look away.

Not startled.
Not flustered.

Just…
considering.

Like someone who had seen far worse things than a reckless man with a sharp smile and no sense of self-preservation.

“…That’s unfortunate,” he said at last.

Chōbei’s grin widened.

“Yeah?”

A pause.

Then—
softly—

“It usually is.”

And still—
he didn’t leave.

Neither of them did.

The moon watched.

And something dangerous—
something mutual—
took its first breath.

