One-Shot II: Teeth and Moonlight
Chōbei didn’t come back quietly.
He never did anything quietly.

The second night, he didn’t bother with the trees.
Didn’t slip through shadows.
Didn’t pretend.

He walked straight up the stone path like he owned it.

“…Oi,” he called out lazily, hands tucked into his sleeves, “Moon Prince.”

The title was mocking.
Invented.
Disrespectful.

He grinned when he said it.

On the terrace—
Tsukihiko didn’t look up immediately.

Brush.
Water.
Ink.

A deliberate pause.

Then—
“…You came back.”

Not surprised.

Chōbei’s grin sharpened.
“Yeah. Thought I’d see if you were real or just something I made up.”

Tsukihiko finally lifted his gaze.
Grey eyes steady.

“And?” he asked.

Chōbei stepped onto the terrace again.
Closer than last time.
More certain.

“…Still deciding.”

A beat.

Tsukihiko dipped his brush again.
Unbothered.

“You’re loud again,” he said.

Chōbei huffed a laugh.
“You like it.”

“No,” Tsukihiko replied.
Pause.

“…but I tolerate it.”

That—
that made Chōbei’s eyes light up.

“Careful,” he said, circling slightly now—not aimless, not wandering.
Testing.
Looking.

“Sounds like you’re getting used to me.”

Tsukihiko didn’t follow him with his head.
But his awareness did.

“Not yet.”

Chōbei clicked his tongue.
“Shame.”

He stopped behind him.
Close.

Too close.

Close enough to see the fine movement of his hand as the brush glided across paper.
Close enough to smell clean ink and night air.

“…You always this calm,” Chōbei murmured, voice lower now, curiosity threading through it, “or just pretending?”

Tsukihiko didn’t turn.

“If I were pretending,” he said evenly, “you would already be gone.”

Chōbei’s grin went feral.

“Yeah?” he leaned in slightly, just enough to test the boundary.
“Then why am I still here?”

A pause.

Then—
Tsukihiko set the brush down.

Slowly.

Carefully.

He turned.

And now—
they were close.

No distance.
No buffer.

“You haven’t done anything worth removing yet,” Tsukihiko said.

Flat.
Honest.

Chōbei stared at him for a second—
then laughed.

“Wow,” he said, delighted. “So I gotta earn it?”

“…If you’re capable.”

That hit.

Chōbei stepped closer.

Chest to chest now.

Deliberate.
Provocative.

“You talk big,” he said, voice dropping, eyes sharp, searching for a crack, a reaction, anything.

Tsukihiko didn’t move.
Didn’t lean back.
Didn’t brace.

Nothing.

“…You push too much,” Tsukihiko replied.

“Yeah?” Chōbei’s hand came up—
fast—
grabbing a loose strand of that long white hair, letting it slide through his fingers.

Testing.
Possessive.
Intrusive.

“Then stop me.”

The world shifted.

Not loudly.
Not violently.

Just—
decisively.

Chōbei didn’t even fully register the movement.

One second—
he was standing over him.

The next—
his wrist was caught.

Turned.

His body twisted with it—
forced down—
hard.

Stone met his back.

Breath knocked from his lungs.

And Tsukihiko was there.

Above him.

Knee pinning his arm.
Grip precise.
Unshakable.

Not angry.

Worse—
controlled.

“…I told you,” Tsukihiko said quietly, looking down at him, expression unchanged, “you push too much.”

Chōbei blinked—
then—

grinned.

Wide.
Sharp.
Thrilled.

“…There it is,” he breathed.

He tested the hold.
Pulled.
Twisted.

Didn’t budge.

“Not bad,” he added, laughing under his breath. “Not bad at all.”

Tsukihiko’s grip tightened—
just slightly.

Enough to remind.

“I don’t repeat myself,” he said.

“Good,” Chōbei shot back immediately, eyes bright, alive, lit up in a way that had nothing to do with fear.
“I don’t listen anyway.”

A beat.

Then—
Tsukihiko released him.

Just like that.

No lingering.
No hesitation.

Chōbei rolled to sit up, flexing his wrist, still grinning like he’d just found something he didn’t know he was looking for.

“…You’re fun,” he said.

Tsukihiko returned to his place.
Picked up the brush again.

“You’re still here,” he replied.

Chōbei barked a laugh.

“Yeah,” he said, watching him now—really watching.

Not just the beauty.
Not just the calm.

The control.
The restraint.
The fact that he could have done worse—
and chose not to.

“…You didn’t kill me,” Chōbei added.

Tsukihiko didn’t look up.

“You didn’t earn it.”

That—
that settled something.

Chōbei leaned back on his hands, looking up at the moon now instead of him.

“…Then I’ll stick around,” he said casually.

A pause.

“…See what it takes.”

Tsukihiko’s brush moved again.
Smooth.
Precise.

“…Do what you want.”

But there—
just barely—

a shift.

Allowance.

Not permission.

Something more dangerous.

Chōbei smirked to himself.

“Yeah,” he muttered.

“I will.”

And this time—
he didn’t leave right away.

