V. What Is Chosen, What Is Taken
The estate was quieter at night in a different way than the forest.
Not empty.
Not still.
Just… held.

Tsukihiko found her where he always did when she wanted space but not solitude.

The inner garden.

Moonlight filtered through thin branches, catching on the surface of the water, turning it silver. The air carried the faint scent of night-blooming flowers.
Leora sat at the edge of the pool, one leg drawn up, the other stretched out, absently trailing her fingers through the water.

She didn’t look up when he approached.

“You’re thinking too loud,” she said.

Tsukihiko stopped just behind her.
A faint exhale left him.

“…You always know.”

“Of course I do.”

A beat.

“…Sit,” she added.

He did.
Close—but not pressed. Familiar. Easy.

For a moment, neither of them spoke.

Then—
“…there’s someone,” Tsukihiko said.

Leora huffed softly.
Amused already.

“There’s always someone,” she replied. “What’s different about this one?”

Tsukihiko’s gaze drifted to the water.
Watching the ripples settle.

“He doesn’t leave.”

That got her attention.

She glanced at him now, sharp, assessing.

“…Ah.”

A pause.

“…And you haven’t made him.”

“No.”

Another pause.

“…Why not?”

Tsukihiko considered that.
Carefully.

“…I wanted to.”

Honest.

Leora’s mouth curved slightly.

“Of course you did.”

“…But I didn’t,” he continued.

Now that made her turn fully toward him.

“Why?”

Tsukihiko’s eyes flicked to hers.
Grey. Steady.

“…because I wasn’t sure if that was me.”

Silence.

Leora stilled.

And then—
slowly—
she smiled.

Not soft.
Not gentle.

Proud.

“…good,” she said quietly.

Tsukihiko didn’t relax.
Not fully.

“He pushes,” he added. “He takes space. He doesn’t ask.”

Leora snorted.

“Sounds familiar.”

“…Yes.”

A beat.

“…I don’t mind it.”

There it was.

Leora’s eyes sharpened slightly.

“No,” she said. “You wouldn’t.”

Another pause.

“…but I don’t know if I should.”

That—
that made her laugh.
Low.
Soft.
Knowing.

“Oh, that’s a dangerous question,” she murmured.

Tsukihiko tilted his head slightly.

“…why.”

Leora leaned back on her hands, looking up at the moon now.

“Because ‘should’ has never stopped you,” she said. “Or him.”

A beat.

“…or your father.”

Tsukihiko didn’t react outwardly.
But something in him stilled.

“…He’s not like you,” he said.

Leora hummed.

“No,” she agreed. “He wouldn’t be.”

Another pause.

“…does he scare you?”

Tsukihiko shook his head.

“No.”

“…does he make you feel watched?”

A flicker.

“…yes.”

“…good.”

Tsukihiko glanced at her.

“…good?”

Leora smiled slightly.

“You should be watched,” she said. “Not owned. Not cornered. But seen.”

A pause.

“…and do you feel owned?”

“No.”

“…cornered?”

“No.”

“…then what’s the problem?”

Tsukihiko looked down at his hands.
Long fingers.
Steady.

“…he would take,” he said. “If I let him.”

Leora didn’t miss a beat.

“Then don’t let him,” she said simply.

Silence.

“…it’s not that simple.”

She laughed again.

“It is that simple. It’s just not easy.”

A pause.

Then—
she leaned slightly closer, nudging his shoulder with hers.

“You’re not your father,” she said.

Tsukihiko’s gaze flicked to her.

“…and you’re not me.”

A beat.

“…so don’t play it like we would.”

He exhaled slowly.

“…what would you do?”

Leora’s smile turned sharp.

“Exactly what you’re doing,” she said. “Let him come. Let him push. And when he crosses a line—”

Her eyes flashed.

“—make him regret it.”

A pause.

“…but don’t punish him for wanting you.”

That—
that settled something.

Tsukihiko nodded once.

“…and if I want him too?”

Leora didn’t even hesitate.

“Then you’d better be honest about it,” she said. “Or you’ll end up hurting him worse than your father ever hurt me.”

Silence.

Heavy.

Real.

And above them—
unseen—

Sukuna stood.

Still.

Listening.

His gaze was fixed on Tsukihiko.
Sharp.
Measuring.

Not angry.
Not yet.

But something dark flickered beneath it.

“…he’s aware,” Sukuna murmured.

At his side—
a smaller presence shifted.

Mizuki.

Barely contained energy, sharp eyes gleaming with interest.

“He’s thinking about it,” Mizuki said, almost delighted. “Can I go see him again?”

Sukuna’s gaze didn’t move.

“…no.”

A pause.

“…watch.”

Mizuki grinned.

Wide.
Dangerous.

“Oh, I will.”

Below—
Tsukihiko rose.

“…thank you,” he said quietly.

Leora waved him off.

“Don’t thank me,” she said. “Just don’t be stupid.”

A faint smile touched his mouth.

“…I won’t.”

And as he walked back toward the terrace—
toward the place where Chōbei always returned—

Sukuna watched him go.

Silent.

Calculating.

“…he’s choosing,” Mizuki said softly.

Sukuna’s eyes narrowed slightly.

“…yes.”

A beat.

“…let’s see if the boy survives it.”

